{ Both queens are already active near the centre, and he still has a knight to bring out. } 14. Nd2 { He drops his knight back to d two, and only his queen still keeps watch over it. } e5 { A little pawn attacks his queen. Even a pawn can be the deflector. [%cal Ge5d4] } (14... Rd8 { A useful looking rook move, and the chance has gone. } 15. Bf3 Qa6 16. e4 e5 { Now the push comes too late. }) (14... Qh5 { The queen steps aside and he untangles. } 15. Nf3 Qg4 16. Qxg4 Nxg4 { A level game. }) (14... Qg4 { You offer a trade with something much better available. } 15. f4 Bd7 16. Nf3 Rfd8 { The knight on d two is still sitting there, guarded and safe — you never went after it. }) 15. Qxe5 { She grabs the pawn, but that means the knight on d two is on its own now. } Qxd2 { The knight has no cover left, and you scoop it up for nothing. } (15... Re8 { You chase the queen instead of taking the knight. } 16. Qd4 Bf5 17. a5 Rac8 { The knight escaped and the win with it. }) (15... Bg4 { A pin looks clever, but the knight was free. } 16. Qd4 Bf5 17. h3 Rfd8 { He has consolidated. }) 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: deflection, do not take the queen"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/qDdKT3lT"]
[FEN "1r2kb1r/3q1ppp/1B2pn2/8/8/5Q2/PP3PPP/3R1RK1 b k - 6 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1r2kb1r/3q1ppp/1B2pn2/8/8/5Q2/PP3PPP/3R1RK1_b_k_-_6_21"]

{ Both queens are still on the board, and his rook still has a job to do on b eight. } 21... Qb7 { He offers to trade queens. Do not take it — ask what his rook on b eight is doing first. } 22. Rd8+ { You go to the square his rook is guarding. He has to take. [%csl Rb8] } (22. Qxb7 { You take the queen, and the rook takes back for free. } 22... Rxb7 23. Bd4 Be7 24. a4 O-O { All that is left is a level endgame. }) (22. Bd4 { A nice square, but he grabs your queen first. } 22... Qxf3 23. gxf3 Nd5 24. Rfe1 Kd7 { You lost the thread and the game. }) 22... Rxd8 { The rook has left b eight. What was it guarding over there? } 23. Qxb7 { The queen is yours. Check pulled his rook off b eight, and the queen it was guarding falls right behind it — deflection. } (23. Bxd8 { The wrong capture, and his queen takes yours. } 23... Qxf3 24. gxf3 Kxd8 25. Rd1+ Kc7 { From winning to worse in one move. }) (23. Qxf6 { A knight instead of a queen. } 23... gxf6 24. Bxd8 Kxd8 25. Rd1+ Kc7 { Count before you take. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the decoy, the lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/1AYjl6wU"]
[FEN "r2k3r/ppbn1ppp/8/3N4/8/7P/PPP2PP1/R3R1K1 b - - 0 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2k3r/ppbn1ppp/8/3N4/8/7P/PPP2PP1/R3R1K1_b_-_-_0_17"]

{ Next trick: the decoy. It drags a piece to a square where something bad is waiting for it there. } 17... Re8 { His rook challenges yours on the e file. Take it with check on e eight — that drags his king to a square where a fork is waiting. [%cal Ye1e8][%csl Rc7] } 18. Rxe8+ { He must take with the king, and that square is not a safe one. [%csl Rd8] } (18. Red1 { You keep the rook and the whole idea is gone. } 18... Re6 19. Rd2 Rc8 20. Rad1 Rd6 { The rooks come off even, and the king never had to walk anywhere near your knight. }) (18. c4 { A quiet pawn move while a fork was waiting. } 18... Rxe1+ 19. Rxe1 Bd6 20. b4 b6 { Nothing left here. }) 18... Kxe8 { Now his king and his bishop stand on two forking squares. [%csl Re8,Rc7] } 19. Nxc7+ { Check, and a free bishop. That is a decoy: bring him where you want him. } (19. Re1+ { A check, but the fork was the whole point. } 19... Kd8 20. Re7 Rc8 21. Rxf7 Be5 { A pawn instead of a piece. }) (19. Rd1 { A calm move, and the fork disappears. } 19... Bd8 20. c3 Nb6 21. Nxb6 axb6 { Trading the knight off leaves nothing but an equal ending — the fork is long gone. }) 1-0


