[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r7/2pk2p1/2b1p3/4PP1p/7P/3BK1P1/8/2R5_b_-_-_0_34"]

{ The position looks drawn at a glance, both sides down to a rook, a bishop and a handful of pawns. } 34... Ra3 { His rook swings out to a three, but his king is still just one square away from disaster. } 35. fxe6+ { The pawn drags his king to e six. Now look down the c column. [%csl Rc6] } (35. Kf4 { A safe king step, and the position is dead level. } 35... Bd5 36. Be2 exf5 37. Bxh5 c6 { The king steps to safety and the pawns trade off — nothing about the position has changed. }) (35. Ke2 { You step out of the pin and the moment is gone. } 35... Bf3+ 36. Kd2 Bd5 37. f6 gxf6 { The king slips out through e two, and the position settles back to equal. }) 35... Kxe6 { He had to take. Now look at what his king and his bishop share. [%csl Re6] } 36. Rxc6+ { The rook takes the bishop with check. The decoy did all the work. } (36. Kf4 { You forgot about the check on c six. } 36... Ra4+ 37. Rc4 Rxc4+ 38. Bxc4+ Ke7 { All that work for a draw. }) (36. Kd4 { A king move with a free bishop on the board. } 36... Bd5 37. Rxc7 Ra4+ 38. Ke3 Ra3 { You grab a pawn back, but giving his bishop time to plant on d five lets him hold the balance. }) 1-0


