{ Both kings are stripped bare in this ending, and you still have a rook that he does not. } 41... Qxd2 { His queen grabs the rook on d two, but that leaves his king dangerously alone in the open. Punish him. } 42. Bb4+ { The bishop offers itself to pull the king off the defence of his queen. [%cal Gd5d2] } (42. Qe5+ { A check, but he blocks and keeps everything. } 42... Qd4 43. Qe1+ Kc2 44. Bg5 Qd6+ { You are a rook down and lost. }) (42. Qf7 { You save the queen and stay a rook behind. } 42... Bd4 43. Qf1 Be5+ 44. Kh1 Kb2 { There was a way, and this was not it. }) 42... Kxb4 { The king had to take, and now he is a long way from home. [%csl Rb4] } 43. Qxd2+ { Check, and the queen. The bishop was the decoy: it dragged his king to b four, and your queen collects on d two. } (43. Qf7 { The queen wanders off and the game is over. } 43... Qxa2 44. Qxg7 Qc2 45. Qe7+ c5 { You are simply lost. }) (43. a3+ { A check that does nothing at all. } 43... Kc3 44. Qe5+ Kc2 45. a4 Bd4 { Still a rook down. }) 1-0


