{ Both queens are the last heavy pieces left near the kings, with a rook and a knight still on the board too. } 45. Qf2 { He offers to trade queens. Taking is fine — finding the right order first is much better. } Rh2+ { The rook goes first. The king must take, and then he stands on h two. } (45... Qxf2+ { The trade first, and all the magic is gone. } 46. Kxf2 Rh2+ 47. Ke1 c3 48. Kd1 { Just an endgame, and you had far more. }) (45... Qf5 { A solid defensive move with a win on the board. } 46. Qxf5 gxf5 47. Bb2 Kg6 { Only a draw now. }) 46. Kxh2 { He has to grab the rook — it is check, and there is nothing else to do. } Qxf2+ { A queen for a rook — that decoy check dragged his king onto her line. } (46... Qc6 { You keep the queens on and lose everything. } 47. Bb2 Qd5 48. Kg2 Kg8 { A rook down for nothing. }) (46... Qd8 { A retreat with the point one move away. } 47. Nxe4 Qh8+ 48. Kg1 f5 { Your rook is gone. }) 0-1


