{ Now interference: put your own piece on the defender's line, and it goes blind to what it was guarding. } 24... Bg3 { His bishop jumps to g three, deep in your position. Rook to f three cuts the line his queen uses to watch it. [%cal Yf1f3][%csl Rg3] } 25. Rf3 { His queen guarded the bishop along the third rank. Your rook cuts it. [%cal Re3g3] } (25. Bc5 { You attack the queen and she simply trades. } 25... Qb3 26. Qxb3 Rxb3 27. Rf3 Rb1+ { An equal ending instead of a piece. }) (25. Bd6 { A pretty square with nothing behind it. } 25... Qb6 26. Qc3 Qb5 27. Ra1 Bxf4 { He is fine and your pawn has gone. }) 25... Qd4 { The queen steps away, and she can no longer see g three. [%csl Rg3] } 26. Rxg3 { A whole bishop. Your rook cut the queen's line, and the bishop she was defending is yours for nothing. That is interference. } (26. Qc1 { You defend and forget the bishop. } 26... Bh4 27. Kh2 h5 28. Qg1 Qxg1+ { The bishop got away. }) (26. Qc5 { A trade offer with a free piece on the board. } 26... Qxc5 27. Bxc5 Rb1+ 28. Bg1 Bh4 { You traded queens instead of collecting the loose bishop on g three — it is still sitting there for him. }) 1-0


