{ A knight is about to leap deep into your position, with only one piece watching over it. } 12. Nb6 { His knight lands on b six, and only his queen on d four still defends it. } c5 { A little pawn steps between the queen and her knight. [%cal Rd4b6] } (12... Ra7 { You defend the rook, and the knight is perfectly fine. } 13. Nd5 Rb7 14. Nf4 Qf6 { He is doing well. }) (12... Nd7 { You develop and let the knight eat the rook. } 13. Nxa8 Qxa8 14. Nxc4 d5 { A rook for nothing. }) (12... d5 { A pawn push, but the wrong pawn. } 13. Nxa8 Nd7 14. O-O Qxa8 { The rook has gone. }) 13. Qe3 { She steps back to e three to dodge the pawn, but your pawn on c five still blocks her view of b six. } Qxb6 { She had to leave the diagonal, and your pawn still blocks her way back — that interference costs him the knight. } (13... Ra7 { Careful play, but the knight was free. } 14. Nbxc4 Nc6 15. Qg3 Nd4 { You let him out. }) (13... Nc6 { You develop, and the knight escapes with your rook. } 14. Nxa8 Nd4 15. Kd1 Be7 { A rook down. }) 0-1


