{ A bishop is about to drop onto a square that only the queen, far along the seventh row, still watches. } 18... Be7 { His bishop drops back to e seven, guarded only by his queen along that row. } 19. Rd7 { Your rook stands in the middle of the rank. The queen is cut off. [%cal Rb7e7] } (19. Bh6 { You go after the pawn and forget the bishop. } 19... Qb8 20. Bf4 Qe8 21. Qg4 f5 { Equal, and the trick has gone. }) (19. Qg3 { A good square, but he simply defends. } 19... Bf6 20. Bg5 Bxg5 21. Qxg5 h6 { The bishops come off even, and nothing is left of the trick you were hoping for. }) 19... Qa6 { She runs to a six. Something she was minding is now on its own. [%csl Ra6] } 20. Rxe7 { A free bishop, and your rook is safe there too. Standing on d seven is interference — his queen's line down the row is cut, so you take for nothing. } (20. Bf4 { A developing move with a free piece on the board. } 20... Rae8 21. h4 Qxa2 22. Be5 Qa5 { You let it slip away. }) (20. Qxc6 { A pawn instead of a bishop. } 20... Bf6 21. a3 Qa5 22. g3 Qxc3 { You gave back everything. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the in between move, the lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/EgygDkDv"]
[FEN "1k1r1r2/pp4p1/6q1/2Qp4/5bP1/2P4p/PPN3NP/R4R1K w - - 0 30"]
[SetUp "1"]
