{ His pawn on d three is about to fall to your knight, and his own bishop has a check of its own waiting across the board. } 15... Nxd3 { He grabs the pawn on d three with his knight. Before you take back, is there something bigger? } 16. Bxe6+ { A check first. His knight is not going anywhere. [%csl Rc8] } (16. cxd3 { You take back at once, and he takes your bishop. } 16... Bxb3 17. Kg1 Re8 18. Qg4+ Be6 { A piece for a pawn, the wrong way round. }) (16. Kg1 { You tuck the king away and let him keep it all. } 16... Nxb2 17. Bd4 Nh6 18. Rb1 Nf5 { Two pawns down and no attack. }) 16... fxe6 { The pawn recaptures the bishop — it is the only way to answer the check. } 17. cxd3 { Now the knight, and the pawn you grabbed first makes the difference. That in between check leaves you a clean pawn up, with nothing given back. } (17. Qe4 { You aim at the knight instead of taking it. } 17... Ne5 18. b4 Qa6+ 19. b5 Qa5 { The knight got away. }) (17. b4 { A pawn push with a free knight on the board. } 17... Qf5+ 18. Kg1 Ne5 19. Bxa7 Nf6 { You had it, and let it go. }) 1-0


