{ A bishop is about to grab a pawn on d five, and only one of his pieces could ever hope to take it back. } 10... Bxd5 { He scoops up your pawn on d five with the bishop. Do not grab it back yet. } 11. Bxf6 { Take the knight first. His queen has to answer that. [%csl Rf6] } (11. Rxd5 { The obvious recapture, and he is completely fine. } 11... Nxd5 12. Bxg7 Rg8 13. Bh6 Nc6 { You traded into nothing. }) (11. g4 { A wild pawn push while a piece hangs. } 11... O-O 12. b3 a5 13. Rg1 a4 { You are just a pawn down. }) 11... Qxf6 { She has to recapture the knight — nothing else covers f six. } 12. Rxd5 { And now the bishop, safe as houses: his own queen on e two pins his e six pawn, so nothing can take it back. That is the in between idea — take the piece going nowhere before the one that might escape. } (12. Qe3 { You defend instead of taking. } 12... O-O 13. h4 Nd7 14. h5 h6 { The bishop walked away. }) (12. Kb1 { A safe king move with a free bishop waiting. } 12... Bb7 13. Qc4 Nc6 14. Bd3 O-O-O { You lost the piece. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: in between, grab the rook"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/xsH9QpS3"]
[FEN "2k4r/b1p5/2P2pp1/1p6/4P1p1/2Bp2Pr/PP3PK1/2R4R b - - 1 33"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2k4r/b1p5/2P2pp1/1p6/4P1p1/2Bp2Pr/PP3PK1/2R4R_b_-_-_1_33"]

{ His bishop on a seven is eyeing your pawn on f two, and his rook is already deep in your camp on h three. } 33... Bxf2 { His bishop grabs your pawn on f two. Taking back looks obvious — look wider first. } 34. Rxh3 { Take the rook first. That bishop cannot run away. [%csl Rh3] } (34. Kxf2 { The automatic recapture, and the rooks come off in his favour. } 34... Rxh1 35. Rxh1 Rxh1 36. Ke3 f5 { Now you are the one losing. }) (34. Bd2 { You save the bishop and both rooks disappear. } 34... Rxh1 35. Rxh1 Rxh1 36. Kxh1 Bxg3 { Nothing left at all. }) 34... gxh3+ { He recaptures your rook with the pawn, and it comes with check. } 35. Kxf2 { And now the bishop. The rooks traded off even, so grabbing his first cost you nothing — the in between move leaves you up a piece for a pawn. } (35. Kh2 { You dodge the check and forget the bishop. } 35... Be3 36. Rd1 d2 37. Bxf6 Re8 { His passed pawn is running. }) (35. Kf3 { The king hunts the wrong way. } 35... h2 36. Kxf2 h1=Q 37. Rxh1 Rxh1 { You gave him a new queen. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the trapped piece, the lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/PZImD4rt"]
[FEN "r2qk2r/1pp3p1/p1nbbn1p/3p1p2/3P4/PQN1PN1P/1PPB1P2/R3KB1R w KQkq - 0 13"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qk2r/1pp3p1/p1nbbn1p/3p1p2/3P4/PQN1PN1P/1PPB1P2/R3KB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_13"]

{ Last trick: the trapped piece. When something has no square left to run to, you do not need to win it by force — you just take it. } 13. Qxb7 { His queen dives in and grabs your pawn on b seven. Knight to a five traps her there. [%cal Yc6a5] } Na5 { The knight attacks her, and every escape square is covered. [%csl Rb7] } (13... Ra7 { You chase her and she simply leaves with a pawn. } 14. Qb3 O-O 15. Rg1 Qe7 { She slips out to b three with an extra pawn in her pocket, and the trap is empty. }) (13... Bd7 { You defend the knight, and she strolls home. } 14. Qb3 Be6 15. Qa4 Bd7 { Defending the knight lets her calmly walk home to a four — you spent a move and she is safe. }) (13... Ne7 { A quiet knight move and the moment is gone. } 14. Ne5 O-O 15. f4 Rb8 { She is out and a pawn up. }) 14. Qxa6 { She has no safe square left, so she grabs one more pawn on her way down. } Rxa6 { [%tail] And there she goes — a whole queen falls to the knight that gave her no square. } (14... O-O { You castle and let her escape with the pawn. } 15. Qe2 Nc4 16. Ne5 Nxe5 { A whole queen, gone. }) (14... Nc4 { You attack her again, but she is already free. } 15. Qb5+ c6 16. Qb7 Nxd2 { Nothing left of the trap. }) 15. Bxa6 { His other bishop scoops the rook back, so he keeps a rook and two pawns for the queen. [%cal Gf1a6] } Qa8 { Your queen swings to a eight, adding pressure down the long diagonal. A queen for a rook and two pawns — the trapped piece still leaves you clearly ahead. } 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the rook in the corner"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/471AM7KL"]
[FEN "rn1qk2r/ppp2ppp/5b2/3P4/4P3/5b1P/PPP1QPP1/RN2KB1R w KQkq - 0 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rn1qk2r/ppp2ppp/5b2/3P4/4P3/5b1P/PPP1QPP1/RN2KB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_10"]

{ A rook is boxed into the corner by his own pawn and knight, with nowhere at all to run. } 10. Qxf3 { He grabs your bishop on f three with the queen, but his own pieces still have that rook in the corner walled in. } Bxb2 { The bishop walks in. The rook on a one has nowhere safe to go. [%csl Ra1] } (10... O-O { Safe and sensible, but the rook was there for the taking. } 11. Nc3 Re8 12. Bc4 Qd6 { A normal game. }) (10... Nd7 { You develop, and he defends the corner. } 11. c3 O-O 12. Nd2 Rb8 { The chance has gone. }) 11. Qb3 { She slides over to attack the bishop on b two, but his own pieces still leave that rook nowhere to go. } Bxa1 { The rook is yours. A trapped piece is worth exactly this: a piece with no squares is a piece you can simply win. } (11... Be5 { You save the bishop and forget the rook. } 12. c3 O-O 13. Nd2 Nd7 { The rook escaped. }) (11... Bf6 { The bishop retreats with a rook on offer. } 12. c3 O-O 13. Nd2 Nd7 { You had a whole rook. }) (11... Qf6 { You defend, and he grabs on b seven. } 12. Qxb7 O-O 13. Qxa8 Bxa1 { A much smaller catch. }) 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: trap the queen"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/kLwPDHWx"]
[FEN "r3k2r/pp1n1pp1/4p3/3pPnPp/2pP1Q1P/2B2N2/qPPK1P2/3R3R b kq - 5 19"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3k2r/pp1n1pp1/4p3/3pPnPp/2pP1Q1P/2B2N2/qPPK1P2/3R3R_b_kq_-_5_19"]

{ A queen is stranded deep in enemy territory, and her escape squares are about to run out. } 19... g6 { He tucks in a pawn move, but his queen is still stuck deep in your camp on a two. Count the squares she has. } 20. Ra1 { The rook attacks her, and there is nowhere for her to go. [%csl Ra2] } (20. Rb1 { Almost right. But from b one she slips out to a four. } 20... Qa4 21. Ra1 Qb5 22. Ng1 b6 { She got away. }) (20. Ng1 { A knight retreat, and she simply runs. } 20... a5 21. Ne2 Qa4 22. Ra1 Qb5 { She is safe now. }) 20... Qxa1 { She has no safe square left, so she grabs the rook instead of nothing. } 21. Rxa1 { She had to take the rook, and you take her straight back. That is the trapped piece again: a queen for a rook. } (21. Ng1 { You forget to take her back. } 21... Qa4 22. Ne2 O-O 23. Ng3 Ng7 { A whole rook for nothing. }) (21. Rc1 { The wrong rook, and the queen lives. } 21... Qa4 22. Qh2 O-O 23. Qh1 Qc6 { You had her. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: take her last square"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/z0yyRVrk"]
[FEN "3r1rk1/p2nppbp/2p2np1/qpPp4/N2P1B2/PP1BP2P/5PP1/R2Q1RK1 b - - 0 15"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3r1rk1/p2nppbp/2p2np1/qpPp4/N2P1B2/PP1BP2P/5PP1/R2Q1RK1_b_-_-_0_15"]

{ A queen has wandered a long way from home, and her path back is about to narrow fast. } 15... bxa4 { His pawn grabs your knight on a four, but his queen is still stranded far from safety. } 16. b4 { The pawn takes her squares away. She has exactly one place to go. [%csl Ra5] } (16. Rb1 { You defend the pawn, and she escapes with the knight. } 16... axb3 17. Rxb3 e5 18. Bh2 Ne4 { A piece down for nothing. }) (16. Bg3 { A quiet bishop move while a piece hangs. } 16... axb3 17. Qxb3 e5 18. Qb4 Qc7 { You are simply a knight behind. }) 16... Qb5 { Her last square, and even that one is watched. } 17. Bxb5 { [%tail] Her last square was watched all along, so the bishop takes the queen for free right now. [%cal Gd3b5] } (17. Bd6 { A fancy move with the queen there for the taking. } 17... exd6 18. Bxb5 cxb5 19. Rc1 Rfe8 { You gave a bishop away for nothing. }) (17. Bc7 { You chase the rook and let her out. } 17... Qb7 18. Bxd8 Rxd8 19. f4 Qc7 { A rook, when a queen was waiting. }) 17... cxb5 { He grabs the bishop back with the pawn, but a queen for a bishop is still yours — the trapped piece pays out again. } 18. f3 { You tuck in a quiet pawn move — there is nothing left to calculate. A queen for a bishop, and the game is not close. } 1-0
